From: chrshdl <cwasilei@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Find PortAudio without find_library()

On Linux, ctypes.util.find_library() looks a library up in ldconfig's
cache, or with a C compiler or a linker. A Buildroot target has none of
them, so it returns None and importing sounddevice fails with
"PortAudio library not found", although libportaudio.so.2 is installed.

Fall back to opening the library by its soname, which the dynamic loader
finds on the default search path. Elsewhere nothing changes: where that
fails, the OSError leads to the bundled library on macOS and Windows as
before, and is raised on other systems.

Upstream: not submitted
Signed-off-by: chrshdl <cwasilei@gmail.com>
---
 src/sounddevice.py | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/sounddevice.py b/src/sounddevice.py
--- a/src/sounddevice.py
+++ b/src/sounddevice.py
@@ -70,7 +70,10 @@
         if _libname is not None:
             break
     else:
-        raise OSError('PortAudio library not found')
+        # find_library() needs ldconfig's cache, a C compiler or a
+        # linker, which an embedded system may not have. Leave the
+        # search to the dynamic loader instead, by the library's soname.
+        _libname = 'libportaudio.so.2'
     _lib: ... = _ffi.dlopen(_libname)
 except OSError:
     if _platform.system() == 'Darwin':
-- 
